# bookmarks (rung 2) — the live-model cap, reached.
#
# `bookmarks::app::App` installs a `LimitPolicy` with `maxLiveModels = 256`.
# Its own comment says why: `BookmarksAuthorizer::authorizeRegister` is
# permissive by choice, so an unauthenticated client can make the server
# create model instances even though it can never execute anything useful on
# them once `SigningAuthorizer::authorize` rejects the call.
# `maxLiveModels` is the framework's answer to that shape of churn.
#
# This file is the proof the cap is real: registrations succeed up to 256 live
# models, and the next one is refused with `err "too many models"`. No session
# is installed anywhere in it, because none is needed -- which is the fact the
# cap exists to bound.
#
# ── The arithmetic, which is off by one from the obvious ───────────────
# The `client` directive does three things: it connects, sends `hello`, and
# sends `register`. So the connection already holds **one** instance before
# the first `send register` below, and 255 more reach the cap rather than 256.
# Getting that wrong is how this file was first written, and the server said
# so at the 256th send.
#
# ── Why it is hundreds of lines of the same thing ──────────────────────
# The cap is 256 and the scenario DSL has no loop, so reaching it takes 255
# sends. That length is the honest cost of asserting a real limit instead of
# exempting it in `coverage_allowlist.json` with a note saying it was too
# tedious to drive. The body below was generated; it is committed rather than
# its generator, because a checked-in file is what the runner and the coverage
# report read.
#
# ── Why this rung ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `pastebin` installs no `LimitPolicy` at all, so its server has no cap to
# reach. bookmarks, polls, kanban and ledger all install the same 256; this
# file sits in the first of them.
#
# Not a workflow, and not counted as one: it dispatches no domain action.
#
# ── Re-runnability, which is the interesting part ──────────────────────
# Model instances are held by the *connection*, so closing it releases all of
# them. The driver gives each rung one server for its whole directory and
# `--twice` runs that directory again, so this file has to leave the server
# with the capacity it found. Its closing steps drop the connection, and every
# instance created here goes with it.

model BookmarkModel

# This already registers the first instance.
client filler

# ── 255 more, bringing the total to exactly the cap ────────────────────

send register typeId=BookmarkModel
expect ok field @modelId != 0

send register typeId=BookmarkModel
expect ok

send register typeId=BookmarkModel
expect ok

send register typeId=BookmarkModel
expect ok

send register typeId=BookmarkModel
expect ok

